WEBVTT

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.520
Speaker 1: These videos, I shot them when I was in Canada and it was a busy period.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.480
Speaker 1: The last time, I think sometime two years ago.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.880
Speaker 1: But just in an attempt to stay faithful to the shadow, I shot them.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:35.040
Speaker 1: So they may not have the best quality, but the content is rich, so I hope it blesses you.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:35.920
Speaker 1: Watch this.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.200
Speaker 1: All right, I want to talk about the topic titled was Jesus a feminist?

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:47.520
Speaker 1: And not to get into the argument of the nomenclature and maybe how some people choose to view it.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:48.880
Speaker 1: I know there are different perspectives.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:01:01.790
Speaker 1: All I'm simply saying was, can we from the life of Jesus, see that he, in a very countercultural way, did a lot for the way women were viewed in those days?

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:11.270
Speaker 1: And the answer is an emphatic yes, to the degree that a historian said that one of the best things that happened to women in history was that Jesus was born.

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:12.750
Speaker 1: Well, what did he mean by that?

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:17.590
Speaker 1: I want to start first and foremost from the positioning of the Bible about women.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:36.270
Speaker 1: A very radical, not just theological, but countercultural text in those days is Galatians 3:28, a text that you might have seen many times, but maybe you read it theologically, not understanding the context of that reading and how radical this text actually was at the time.

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:45.630
Speaker 1: And it says, there is neither Jew nor Greek, neither slave nor free, neither male nor female, for you are all one in Christ.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:47.560
Speaker 1: Powerful and radical text.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:49.640
Speaker 1: And why was this counter cultural?

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:52.600
Speaker 1: Because you have to understand the prevalent culture of that day.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:56.120
Speaker 1: So, for instance, let's look at the Greek household.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.480
Speaker 1: How did the Greeks treat their women?

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:03.720
Speaker 1: And this was supposed to be the poster culture of exposure of those days.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:11.360
Speaker 1: The Greeks that were at the forefront of philosophy of music and art and all of that, how did they treat their women?

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:21.050
Speaker 1: Well, well, according to Plutarch, a 2nd century Greek biographer, it was said that the average woman had the social status of his slave.

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:23.090
Speaker 1: It doesn't mean they were slaves.

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:24.810
Speaker 1: It just meant it didn't make a difference.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.010
Speaker 1: They were treated like slaves.

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:27.809
Speaker 1: They were kept in lock and key.

00:02:27.809 --> 00:02:32.090
Speaker 1: You couldn't just go out in your freedom, except you were accompanied by a male slave.

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:40.850
Speaker 1: And in an age that was very big on education, only the boys were allowed to go to school.

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:50.320
Speaker 1: Just imagine that, with a lot to study in poetry, music and gymnastics, only the boys were allowed to go to school and girls were not allowed to go to school at all.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:03:02.880
Speaker 1: I think in all my research, the one that really hit me was the writing of one Hilarion, where his wife, at the point of birth, he sent a message to her.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.600
Speaker 1: And the message read, if it's a male child, let it live.

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:09.980
Speaker 1: If it's a female child, cast it away.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:17.100
Speaker 1: I mean, just imagine that we are not talking about some toy, we're talking about a child.

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:25.980
Speaker 1: So just imagine how bad things were in those days because female children were seen as a social burden and a liability.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.180
Speaker 1: Well, was the Roman culture any different?

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:34.940
Speaker 1: I can argue it was even worse because men had absolute control over their children.

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:38.990
Speaker 1: And I mean absolute to the degree that they were allowed to kill them.

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:39.710
Speaker 1: All right?

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:43.390
Speaker 1: Even married daughters could be killed.

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:48.990
Speaker 1: And when Augustus Caesar came, he reinforced the law and took it a step further.

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:54.270
Speaker 1: Men could kill their wives, especially with suspicions of adultery and all of that.

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:55.390
Speaker 1: Incredible.

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:57.870
Speaker 1: Well, was the Hebrew culture any different?

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:09.560
Speaker 1: You know, one of the things that Jesus kept saying and should stand out to you was that these Jews, they made the word of God of non effect because of their tradition.

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:10.520
Speaker 1: All right?

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:16.839
Speaker 1: When you read, you see that he kept saying that you make, you have made the word of God of none effect because of your tradition.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:24.120
Speaker 1: So they had extreme traditions on the Sabbath, cleansing and things like that, and extreme views on women as well.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:27.240
Speaker 1: I want to read some rabbinical writings, all right?

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:35.240
Speaker 1: One of them said, let the words of the law be burnt rather than being committed to a woman.

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:43.560
Speaker 1: And anyone who teaches his daughter the law, it is as though he taught her lechery, you know, I mean, that was just how they view things.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:52.840
Speaker 1: And so when you understand the tradition, the prevalent tradition in those days, there are some things you read several times that didn't stand out to you that will now stand out to you.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:59.530
Speaker 1: When you see Jesus in Mary and Martha's house and Martha wants to do what her culture allows her to do.

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:01.370
Speaker 1: She just wants to stay in the kitchen and serve.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:05.610
Speaker 1: And Jesus says, no, you're going to stay here and hear the word Martha.

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:07.490
Speaker 1: You are combat about March 7th.

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:09.450
Speaker 1: But Mary has chosen that good part.

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:10.370
Speaker 1: One thing is needful.

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:13.250
Speaker 1: Mary has chosen that good part and shall not be taken away from her.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:16.690
Speaker 1: So that was a radical message to the women.

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:17.650
Speaker 1: Listen to me.

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:26.660
Speaker 1: The message of the gospel is not for men alone because there is neither Jew nor Greek, bond nor free.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:32.340
Speaker 1: There is neither male nor female.

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:39.620
Speaker 1: That was the radical anticultural, I mean countercultural message of Jesus at the time.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:42.180
Speaker 1: You know what else the rabbinic writings had to say?

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:49.060
Speaker 1: One of them wrote and said, he who talks to a woman in public brings evil upon himself.

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:52.810
Speaker 1: One is not so much to as greet her.

00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:56.730
Speaker 1: One is not so much as to greet her.

00:05:57.050 --> 00:05:58.730
Speaker 1: That's one of the writings.

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:02.410
Speaker 1: And so when you now Understand the context.

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:12.250
Speaker 1: You will read John 4 from a different standpoint, that when Jesus engaged the Samaritan woman in a discourse, even she was shocked.

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:21.330
Speaker 1: First and foremost, a Samaritan man will not be engaged by a Jewish man, let alone a Samaritan woman.

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:25.610
Speaker 1: You know, so she was like, you are a Jew and I'm a Samaritan woman.

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:27.730
Speaker 1: The Jews have no dealings with the Gentiles.

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:28.610
Speaker 1: What you're doing.

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:35.650
Speaker 1: And Jesus said, if you knew the gift of God and who it is that was talking to you would have asked him for living water.

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:39.410
Speaker 1: As if to say, you qualify for everything God has to offer.

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:40.610
Speaker 1: Be bold to ask.

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:42.860
Speaker 1: Don't let anyone count you out.

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:47.620
Speaker 1: Listen to every woman listening to me.

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:56.380
Speaker 1: I believe I echo the sentiments of God and the Bible when I tell you, listen, you can go ahead and fulfill God's call for your life.

00:06:56.540 --> 00:06:58.500
Speaker 1: Be content in any way.

00:06:58.500 --> 00:06:59.620
Speaker 1: The Lord is leading you.

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:06.060
Speaker 1: But listen, we have a rich history and a small dashboard of biblical examples.

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:12.050
Speaker 1: To the best of my understanding, they tell you can go ahead and be evangelical disciple people.

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:13.170
Speaker 1: Be a blessing.

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:13.730
Speaker 1: All right?

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:16.810
Speaker 1: Because there is neither male nor female.

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:29.490
Speaker 1: All right, so all of these radical texts tell us, you know, how Jesus saw women and how he supported women.

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:31.330
Speaker 1: I mean, think about it.

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:39.040
Speaker 1: The Bible categorically tells us that when his disciples came back in John 4:27, they were amazed.

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:41.400
Speaker 1: They marveled that he talked with a woman.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:42.760
Speaker 1: They marveled.

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:44.760
Speaker 1: That was the specific thing the Bible says.

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:52.040
Speaker 1: They marveled that they talked to the woman, which sponsors my suspicion that in the first place, he wasn't exactly hungry.

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:58.600
Speaker 1: He knew that they would allow their cultural bias to inhibit his evangelism to the woman.

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:03.260
Speaker 1: And that is why he sent them away, sat by the well to talk to that woman.

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:05.340
Speaker 1: And by the time they came back, they were done.

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:20.220
Speaker 1: All right, you know, and prophetically, I think this is an indication that some people who are trying to hinder God's move in this generation will be sidelined so that God can do what he wants to do, with whom he wants to do it.

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:25.660
Speaker 1: All right, Feel free to say loud amen, but I believe that's what's going to happen in the name of Jesus.

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:28.650
Speaker 1: The final thing I want to say is this.

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:36.570
Speaker 1: And one of the most important things in the entire Bible, you have to understand that salvation is predicated on something the Bible says.

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:48.250
Speaker 1: In Romans, chapter 10, verses 9 and 10, it says, for if you shall believe in your heart the Lord Jesus and confess with your mouth specifically that God raised him from the dead, you shall be saved.

00:08:48.570 --> 00:09:03.420
Speaker 1: And so it tells you that the entire foundation of salvation, genuine salvation, is predicated on a genuine faith and confession in the resurrection of Christ and the efficacy of the same for the saving of everyone who will believe.

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:13.180
Speaker 1: And so when Jesus rises from the dead and of all people to appear to, he appears to women.

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:17.100
Speaker 1: And he says, go tell Peter and the disciples.

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:19.830
Speaker 1: That's powerful.

00:09:19.990 --> 00:09:33.510
Speaker 1: Did I mention that one of the rabbinical writings said a woman's witness does not count in court, but the first witness of the resurrection of Christ was a woman?

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:36.710
Speaker 1: That was the most countercultural move.

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:38.630
Speaker 1: Countercultural move.

00:09:38.950 --> 00:09:44.760
Speaker 1: And if Jesus was pandering to the sentiments of the culture of that day, he would not have done that.

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:50.880
Speaker 1: And if you know anything about divine kairos, you know that was something deliberate.

00:09:51.680 --> 00:10:04.960
Speaker 1: That the culture of Christianity is hinged on the fact that the first person to preach the Gospels to announce the resurrection of Christ was in fact a woman.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:12.440
Speaker 1: So I want to talk about the controversial verses about women being silent.

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:14.360
Speaker 1: I know someone will be wondering.

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:23.320
Speaker 1: Well, Paul in Galatians 3:28 is saying, There is neither Jew nor Greek, neither slave nor free, neither male nor female, for we are all one in Christ.

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:28.240
Speaker 1: Like Yoruba, people will say, paul, like, really, bro?

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:30.120
Speaker 1: From your mouth, you're the one saying that.

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:37.730
Speaker 1: What about the other controversial texts, like First Corinthians 14:34, where the Bible says Paul was speaking?

00:10:38.050 --> 00:10:46.290
Speaker 1: Let your women keep silent in the churches, for they are not permitted to speak, you know, or text like First Timothy, Chapter 2, Verse 15.

00:10:46.850 --> 00:11:00.770
Speaker 1: Now, these texts will take some wrestling, but I'm going to share with you based on my understanding of the Word of God, standard practice when it comes to Bible interpretation.

00:11:01.010 --> 00:11:05.090
Speaker 1: You see, when it comes to Bible interpretation, you move from what is known to what is unknown.

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:06.340
Speaker 1: Never forget that.

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:08.220
Speaker 1: You move from what is known to what is unknown.

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:11.820
Speaker 1: And so what do we know when we look at Paul's ministry?

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:14.180
Speaker 1: How did he walk with ladies?

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:15.020
Speaker 1: How did he.

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:17.580
Speaker 1: What was his true perspective?

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:20.860
Speaker 1: And let's walk all those fine details.

00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:25.940
Speaker 1: So, number one, Paul clearly tells us that women can do ministry.

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:32.820
Speaker 1: When you look at First Corinthians 11:5, he talks about women prophesying with their hair uncovered, you know, and all of that.

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:35.680
Speaker 1: He's telling them how to go about putting prophecy.

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:38.400
Speaker 1: It tells you that prophecy was a phenomenon.

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:42.080
Speaker 1: All right, so it's a given that women can indeed prophesy.

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:54.720
Speaker 1: Because if he's giving instructions on how women should prophesy, how they should do their hair covering, it means that he didn't mean silence altogether.

00:11:54.720 --> 00:12:02.500
Speaker 1: You cannot in chapter 11 be telling women how to prophesy, and in chapter 14 be telling women to be mute.

00:12:02.900 --> 00:12:04.500
Speaker 1: All together, just a very.

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:06.180
Speaker 1: That's very simple and straightforward.

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:13.140
Speaker 1: The Bible also tells us In Acts chapter 21 that Philip had two daughters who prophesied they were prophets.

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:13.940
Speaker 1: All right.

00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:17.300
Speaker 1: Acts 21:8,9.

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:18.260
Speaker 1: All right.

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:20.820
Speaker 1: They had two daughters who were prophets.

00:12:21.140 --> 00:12:26.420
Speaker 1: Now let's see how Paul commended women who were partners in his ministry.

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:29.500
Speaker 1: Starting From Romans, chapter 16.

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:37.500
Speaker 1: From verse one, it says, Commend Phoebe, your sister, who is a servant in the Lord.

00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:38.380
Speaker 1: Guess what?

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:43.180
Speaker 1: The word translated servant is the same word that is translated deacon.

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:43.780
Speaker 1: Deacon.

00:12:43.780 --> 00:12:57.140
Speaker 1: And the reason this is important is because some people have had this flimsy interpretation of First Timothy, Chapter 3, that says that if anyone desires the post of a bishop or a deacon, he desires a good thing.

00:12:57.140 --> 00:12:58.620
Speaker 1: He must be husband of one wife.

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:00.380
Speaker 1: Because he says he must be husband of one wife.

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:04.220
Speaker 1: They say that means implicitly that he must not be a woman.

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:09.820
Speaker 1: Well, Phoebe was a dickiness.

00:13:10.700 --> 00:13:11.899
Speaker 1: It was not a scandal.

00:13:11.899 --> 00:13:12.740
Speaker 1: It was not a secret.

00:13:12.740 --> 00:13:13.980
Speaker 1: She was a deaconess.

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:15.580
Speaker 1: So dead that thought.

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:17.980
Speaker 1: All right, that's very important.

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:20.220
Speaker 1: It doesn't stop there.

00:13:20.540 --> 00:13:25.620
Speaker 1: He goes on in verses three and four, he says, greet Priscilla and Aquila, my fellow workers in Christ.

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:30.200
Speaker 1: See, I don't even know where to start in explaining this to you.

00:13:30.440 --> 00:13:36.040
Speaker 1: First and foremost, it is noteworthy that he mentions the woman first.

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:37.400
Speaker 1: He mentions Priscilla.

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:38.280
Speaker 1: Let me tell you something.

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:46.840
Speaker 1: It is unimaginable that in that context, and in those days, this will be done by accident.

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:54.840
Speaker 1: If he mentions the woman first, it implies that the woman was more prominent in the ministry.

00:13:55.530 --> 00:13:57.370
Speaker 1: And don't take my word for it.

00:13:57.610 --> 00:14:15.850
Speaker 1: When you look at Acts chapter 18 and the role that they played in expounding the word of God to Apollos, the popular Apollos, it tells you that this woman wasn't just helping them arrange the chair, she was a teacher.

00:14:16.010 --> 00:14:21.050
Speaker 1: The Bible clearly says they expounded the word of God more perfectly to him.

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:22.280
Speaker 1: Both of them did.

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:25.160
Speaker 1: So they were both teachers.

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:27.000
Speaker 1: Okay, that's number one.

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:29.240
Speaker 1: Number two, fellow workers.

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:32.720
Speaker 1: Other translations say co laborers.

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:36.600
Speaker 1: Paul called a woman a co laborer.

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:45.200
Speaker 1: I mean, maybe we should just turn off this stream right now and it's over because it's called a co laborer.

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:45.840
Speaker 1: Guess what?

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:51.550
Speaker 1: That was the exact same phrase that he used to describe today Timothy, who was a pastor.

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:53.670
Speaker 1: Who was a pastor.

00:14:53.670 --> 00:14:54.270
Speaker 1: And I'm not.

00:14:54.510 --> 00:14:54.990
Speaker 1: I'm.

00:14:55.070 --> 00:15:00.910
Speaker 1: You see, I'm going to leave you to connect the dots, but I just want you to bear that in mind.

00:15:01.790 --> 00:15:09.710
Speaker 1: There are also other controversial conversations, but we can do that later.

00:15:10.030 --> 00:15:10.630
Speaker 1: Verse 7.

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:12.030
Speaker 1: I'll just give you something to think about.

00:15:12.670 --> 00:15:17.010
Speaker 1: This one's controversial and some people disagree, but look at verse seven.

00:15:17.410 --> 00:15:25.570
Speaker 1: Greet Andronicus and Junia, my countrymen and fellow prisoners who are of note amongst the apostles.

00:15:25.570 --> 00:15:32.689
Speaker 1: So now some people believe that this means they were apostles, including Junia, who was woman.

00:15:32.930 --> 00:15:34.730
Speaker 1: Some people believe that.

00:15:34.730 --> 00:15:35.610
Speaker 1: No, that's.

00:15:35.610 --> 00:15:36.530
Speaker 1: That's not true.

00:15:36.770 --> 00:15:44.300
Speaker 1: But whatever be the case, at least at the base level, it just tells you that Junior was notable.

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:47.660
Speaker 1: Junior was notable, all right.

00:15:47.900 --> 00:15:51.500
Speaker 1: These were people who did great work for the Lord.

00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:52.860
Speaker 1: Great work for the Lord.

00:15:53.020 --> 00:15:59.260
Speaker 1: These were people who had churches in their houses and helped people grow in the knowledge of the truth.

00:15:59.260 --> 00:16:01.460
Speaker 1: Bear in mind there were no church buildings there.

00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:03.500
Speaker 1: Churches were held in people's houses.

00:16:03.740 --> 00:16:13.310
Speaker 1: And so when the Bible tells you that Priscilla and Aquila taught Apollos the word, it's no different from church.

00:16:13.550 --> 00:16:17.150
Speaker 1: It's no different because churches were literally in people's houses.

00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:18.270
Speaker 1: Bear that in mind.

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:36.110
Speaker 1: So now, when you see all of this detail, it is sufficient for you to use that to interpret the difficult text, because you can't have so many women who were in ministry who were openly commended by Paul.

00:16:36.490 --> 00:16:38.330
Speaker 1: And then Paul is saying women should be quiet.

00:16:38.490 --> 00:16:39.690
Speaker 1: It's inconsistent.

00:16:39.850 --> 00:16:42.090
Speaker 1: It tells you that something else was happening there.

00:16:42.250 --> 00:16:50.890
Speaker 1: Now, what I'm going to share with you, that leads me to the next principle of Bible interpretation that I want to talk about that will help us in this context.

00:16:50.890 --> 00:16:52.810
Speaker 1: And it's the context rule.

00:16:53.850 --> 00:16:58.730
Speaker 1: So in 1 Corinthians 14, look at it, first and foremost, flow of thought.

00:16:58.810 --> 00:17:06.819
Speaker 1: He's talking about how something good can, if not managed, cause more harm than could cause confusion.

00:17:06.819 --> 00:17:10.140
Speaker 1: He's talking about the administration of spiritual gifts in the church.

00:17:10.940 --> 00:17:13.660
Speaker 1: And he's saying, if you have a message, take turns.

00:17:14.140 --> 00:17:15.180
Speaker 1: Verse 33.

